            "Burns in Ghana come from open fires and kerosene stoves, gas cylinder explosions, hot water and oil in the kitchen, epileptic seizures over cooking fires, electrical contact in informal wiring and on scaffolding, acid attacks, and the fuel tanker and fire disasters that bring twenty patients at once; the Reconstructive Plastic Surgery and Burns Centre at Korle Bu and the burns services in Kumasi and Tamale receive the worst, but every ICU admits burns. The first danger is the <strong>airway</strong>: a burn in an enclosed space, facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in the mouth or sputum, hoarseness, stridor or a swelling neck mean <strong>inhalation injury</strong>, and the airway that is patent on arrival can be impossible to intubate four hours later as the oedema of resuscitation develops — so the patient with these signs is intubated early by the most experienced operator with an uncut tube (the face will swell past the tube's flange), the tube secured with ties that are loosened as the face swells, and bronchoscopy done when available. <strong>Carbon monoxide</strong> poisoning (headache, confusion, coma with a normal-looking pulse oximeter) is treated with 100 per cent oxygen through a non-rebreathing mask or the ventilator until the carboxyhaemoglobin is below 5 per cent; <strong>cyanide</strong> from burning plastics and foam causes unexplained lactic acidosis above 8 mmol/L and is treated with hydroxocobalamin 5 g IV where available. Circumferential full-thickness burns of the chest restrict ventilation and need <strong>escharotomy</strong>; of the limbs, they compromise perfusion within hours, and the nurse's hourly check of distal pulses (a Doppler if needed), capillary refill, sensation and pain on passive movement decides the escharotomy before the limb is lost.",
